Bug: case-sensitive comparison of the hostname. Fix: normalize the hostname to lowercase
 when comparing. Use URL parsing, and if parsing fails, fall back to case-insensitive
 comparison.

Simple fix:

```ts
function sameUrl(a: string, b: string): boolean {
  const na = normalize(a);
  const nb = normalize(b);
  return na === nb;
}

function normalize(url: string): string {
  try {
    const u = new URL(url);
    return u.href.toLowerCase(); // hmm, lowercasing the whole thing affects the query
 too
  } catch {
    return url.toLowerCase();
  }
}
```

Actually, according to RFC 3986, only the scheme and host are case-insensitive. The
 path is case-sensitive. So the best approach is: lowercase only the host (and scheme
). Parse with URL, and compare `u.origin + u.pathname + u.search + u.hash` while lower
casing the host... An easier way: reconstruct using `u.protocol` (already lowercased
 by the URL parser), `u.host` (also lowercased), `u.pathname`, `u.search`, `u.hash
`. In fact, the URL API already lowercases the scheme and host. So just comparing
 `u.href` after parsing will work! `new URL("https://EXAMPLE.com/a").href` → "https
://example.com/a".

Fallback for invalid URLs: case-insensitive comparison of the whole string, or a regex
-based host lowercasing. Keep it simple: try/catch, and in the catch, return the raw
 lowercased value.
